Here's a complete step-by-step guide for Kubespray on Hetzner, covering VM provisioning, Kubespray config, and the Hetzner-native integrations (CCM + CSI driver):

---

## Overview

The full stack you need:

```
hcloud CLI / Terraform        → provision VMs
Kubespray                     → install Kubernetes
hcloud-cloud-controller-manager (CCM) → Hetzner LB, node IPs
hcloud-csi-driver             → Hetzner Volumes (block storage, RWO)
+ NFS or Longhorn             → shared storage (RWX, if needed)
```

---

## Step 1 — Prerequisites

```bash
# Local machine
pip install ansible-core==2.16.x   # Kubespray requires >=2.16.4, <2.17
pip install hcloud                 # Hetzner Python SDK
brew install hcloud                # or: apt install hcloud / download from GitHub

# Kubespray via Ansible Galaxy (recommended — no git clone needed)
ansible-galaxy collection install kubernetes.core
pip install kubernetes
```

Or clone kubespray directly (classic approach):

```bash
git clone https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kubespray.git
cd kubespray
git checkout release-2.25            # latest stable as of 2025
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

---

## Step 2 — Provision Hetzner VMs

```bash
# Authenticate
hcloud context create my-k8s        # paste your API token

# Upload your SSH key
hcloud ssh-key create --name k8s-key --public-key-file ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

# Create a private network for cluster-internal traffic
hcloud network create --name k8s-net --ip-range 10.0.0.0/16
hcloud network add-subnet k8s-net --type cloud --network-zone eu-central --ip-range 10.0.1.0/24

# Control plane (cx22 = 2 vCPU / 4 GB, minimum for k8s)
hcloud server create \
  --name k8s-cp-1 \
  --type cx22 \
  --image ubuntu-22.04 \
  --ssh-key k8s-key \
  --network k8s-net \
  --location nbg1

# Worker nodes (cx32 = 4 vCPU / 8 GB recommended)
for i in 1 2 3; do
  hcloud server create \
    --name k8s-worker-$i \
    --type cx32 \
    --image ubuntu-22.04 \
    --ssh-key k8s-key \
    --network k8s-net \
    --location nbg1
done

# Get IPs
hcloud server list
```

> **Tip:** Use `--location nbg1` (Nuremberg), `hel1` (Helsinki), or `fsn1` (Falkenstein) — all in the EU and very cheap. Hetzner is ~5× cheaper than AWS/GCP for equivalent specs.

---

## Step 3 — Build Kubespray Inventory

```bash
cd kubespray
cp -rfp inventory/sample inventory/hetzner

# Set your IPs (public IPs from hcloud server list)
declare -a IPS=(
  1.2.3.10    # cp-1
  1.2.3.11    # worker-1
  1.2.3.12    # worker-2
  1.2.3.13    # worker-3
)

CONFIG_FILE=inventory/hetzner/hosts.yaml \
  python3 contrib/inventory_builder/inventory.py ${IPS[@]}
```

The generated `hosts.yaml` will look like this — **edit it** to assign roles properly:

```yaml
# inventory/hetzner/hosts.yaml
all:
  hosts:
    k8s-cp-1:
      ansible_host: 1.2.3.10
      ip: 10.0.1.10           # private network IP
      access_ip: 1.2.3.10
    k8s-worker-1:
      ansible_host: 1.2.3.11
      ip: 10.0.1.11
      access_ip: 1.2.3.11
    k8s-worker-2:
      ansible_host: 1.2.3.12
      ip: 10.0.1.12
      access_ip: 1.2.3.12
    k8s-worker-3:
      ansible_host: 1.2.3.13
      ip: 10.0.1.13
      access_ip: 1.2.3.13
  children:
    kube_control_plane:
      hosts:
        k8s-cp-1:
    kube_node:
      hosts:
        k8s-worker-1:
        k8s-worker-2:
        k8s-worker-3:
    etcd:
      hosts:
        k8s-cp-1:          # for HA: add 3 nodes here
    k8s_cluster:
      children:
        kube_control_plane:
        kube_node:
    calico_rr:
      hosts: {}
```

---

## Step 4 — Configure group_vars for Hetzner

**`inventory/hetzner/group_vars/all/all.yml`** — the critical Hetzner settings:

```yaml
# ── Cloud provider (CRITICAL for Hetzner) ────────────────────────────────────
cloud_provider: external
external_cloud_provider: hcloud

# Your Hetzner API token (use ansible-vault in production!)
external_hcloud_cloud:
  hcloud_api_token: "YOUR_HETZNER_API_TOKEN"

# ── Network ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Use private network IPs for cluster communication
kube_apiserver_bind_address: "0.0.0.0"

# ── SSH ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ansible_user: root                  # Hetzner Ubuntu images use root by default
ansible_ssh_private_key_file: ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
ansible_ssh_common_args: '-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no'
```

**`inventory/hetzner/group_vars/k8s_cluster/k8s-cluster.yml`**:

```yaml
# ── Kubernetes version ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
kube_version: v1.29.3

# ── CNI ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Calico works best with Hetzner; Flannel also works
kube_network_plugin: calico

# Pod and service CIDRs (must not overlap with Hetzner private network 10.0.0.0/16)
kube_pods_subnet: 192.168.0.0/16
kube_service_addresses: 10.96.0.0/12

# ── Container runtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
container_manager: containerd

# ── Hetzner cloud controller ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IMPORTANT: required so kubelet doesn't wait for cloud provider node init
node_labels:
  node.cloudprovider.kubernetes.io/uninitialized: "true"

# ── kubeconfig ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Download kubeconfig to your local machine after install
kubeconfig_localhost: true
kubectl_localhost: true

# ── Optional addons ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
helm_enabled: true
metrics_server_enabled: true
cert_manager_enabled: true
ingress_nginx_enabled: true
```

**`inventory/hetzner/group_vars/k8s_cluster/addons.yml`**:

```yaml
# Disable in-tree cloud providers (Hetzner uses external CCM)
cloud_provider: external
```

---

## Step 5 — Run Kubespray

```bash
# Dry run first (check connectivity)
ansible -i inventory/hetzner/hosts.yaml all -m ping

# Full cluster install (~20–30 min on Hetzner cx22)
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hetzner/hosts.yaml \
  --become --become-user=root \
  cluster.yml
```

When done, your kubeconfig will be at `inventory/hetzner/artifacts/admin.conf`.

```bash
export KUBECONFIG=$(pwd)/inventory/hetzner/artifacts/admin.conf
kubectl get nodes
```

---

## Step 6 — hcloud Cloud Controller Manager (CCM)

Kubespray has built-in support for the hcloud external cloud controller manager, but you may need to deploy it manually depending on your version. The CCM enables Hetzner Load Balancers and correct node IP assignment:

```bash
# Create the API token secret
kubectl -n kube-system create secret generic hcloud \
  --from-literal=token=YOUR_HETZNER_API_TOKEN \
  --from-literal=network=k8s-net           # your private network name

# Deploy CCM
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/hetznercloud/hcloud-cloud-controller-manager/releases/latest/download/ccm-networks.yaml
```

After CCM is running, nodes will get their correct Hetzner public IPs and you can use Hetzner Load Balancers via `Service type: LoadBalancer`.

---

## Step 7 — hcloud CSI Driver (Block Storage)

The CCM and CSI driver share the same `hcloud` secret in `kube-system`, so the secret from Step 6 covers both:

```bash
# Deploy hcloud CSI driver
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hetznercloud/csi-driver/main/deploy/kubernetes/hcloud-csi.yml

# Verify StorageClass was created
kubectl get storageclass
# NAME                PROVISIONER         RECLAIMPOLICY
# hcloud-volumes      csi.hetzner.cloud   Delete
```

This gives you **Hetzner Volumes** (block storage, SSD, billed by GB) as PVCs — but **ReadWriteOnce only**. For shared/RWX storage, see below.

---

## Step 8 — Shared Storage Options on Hetzner

Since Hetzner's CSI driver maps to their Cloud Volumes (block storage), RWX requires an additional layer:

| Option | RWX | Complexity | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| NFS server VM | ✅ | Low | ~€4/mo for cx11 |
| **Longhorn** | ✅ (via NFS) | Medium | Uses worker disk space |
| Rook/Ceph | ✅ | High | Needs dedicated disks |

**Longhorn (recommended for Hetzner RWX):**

```bash
helm repo add longhorn https://charts.longhorn.io
helm repo update
helm install longhorn longhorn/longhorn \
  --namespace longhorn-system \
  --create-namespace \
  --set defaultSettings.defaultReplicaCount=2

# Create RWX StorageClass
kubectl apply -f - <<EOF
kind: StorageClass
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
metadata:
  name: longhorn-rwx
provisioner: driver.longhorn.io
allowVolumeExpansion: true
parameters:
  numberOfReplicas: "2"
  accessMode: "ReadWriteMany"  # Longhorn uses NFS internally for RWX
EOF
```

---

## Common Hetzner-specific gotchas

**1. Node stuck `NotReady` after Kubespray** — this is the CCM uninitialized taint. Fix: deploy CCM (Step 6) and the taint clears automatically.

**2. Pod CIDR overlap** — Hetzner private networks use `10.0.0.0/8` range. Set `kube_pods_subnet: 192.168.0.0/16` to avoid conflicts.

**3. `cloud_provider: external` is required** — without it, kubelet uses in-tree cloud detection which doesn't exist for Hetzner. Nodes will never be marked as ready if this is misconfigured.

**4. Hetzner Firewall** — open these ports in the Hetzner console or via `hcloud firewall`:

```bash
hcloud firewall create --name k8s-fw
hcloud firewall add-rule k8s-fw --direction in --protocol tcp --port 22 --source-ips 0.0.0.0/0
hcloud firewall add-rule k8s-fw --direction in --protocol tcp --port 6443 --source-ips 0.0.0.0/0
hcloud firewall add-rule k8s-fw --direction in --protocol all --source-ips 10.0.0.0/16  # private network
hcloud firewall add-rule k8s-fw --direction in --protocol tcp --port 30000-32767 --source-ips 0.0.0.0/0  # NodePorts
```

**5. Ansible version pinning** — Kubespray requires Ansible `>2.16.4` and `<2.17.0`, so pin it explicitly with `pip install ansible-core==2.16.9`.
